Python 3 Migration Notes
========================

This document describes the in-progress Python 3 migration of TMDA
1.1.12. The legacy code, historical copyright notices, license and
existing release identifier are preserved.

Reference implementations
-------------------------

Two independent repositories were reviewed for migration ideas:

* Jonathan Kamens: https://github.com/jikamens/tmda
* Cedric Dufour: https://github.com/cedric-dufour/tmda

The Kamens branch has useful subprocess substitutions but still
imports asyncore, asynchat, md5 and crypt in major components. It
must not be treated as proof of compatibility with Python 3.13+.

The Dufour tmda-1.3.x branch places TMDA in a tmda/ subtree and
includes an extended test suite, an explicit msg_as_bytes helper
and SMTP session byte decoding. It also retains asyncore, asynchat
and crypt, so it is not a direct drop-in replacement on a stock
Python 3.13+ installation.

Compatibility work completed
----------------------------

* HMAC-SHA1 API and original cryptographic cookie representations.
* Python 3-safe text and binary serialization of selected data.
* Removed popen2 functionality in selected utility, parser and
  authentication paths.
* Legacy filter parser string methods and indentation issues.
* Key generation, authentication encoding and CLI smoke tests.
* Python 3 standard-library email instead of the bundled Python 2
  email package on the entry-point path.
* Selectors-based SMTP event loop without pyasyncore/pyasynchat.
* TLS and STARTTLS use ssl.SSLContext rather than the old tlslite
  dependency, with TLS 1.2 as the minimum accepted version.
* Optional legacycrypt fallback for encrypted Unix password files.

Compatibility work still required
---------------------------------

* Production SMTP interoperability, TLS verification policy, STARTTLS
  failure recovery, and proxy transport. Local loopback tests cover
  basic plaintext SMTP, authentication, STARTTLS and implicit TLS.
* Real Maildir and original pending-queue delivery and retrieval.
* Message serialization, MIME and non-ASCII header/body handling.
* FD3/checkpassword process execution and failure handling.
* Compatibility of old file-based password hashes and security policy.
* Integration with supported MTAs and virtual domains.
* Full coverage of bundled helpers, contrib scripts and legacy
  configuration paths.

Existing smoke tests verify command-line startup and compilation,
but do not establish that the SMTP proxy is safe for production.

Migration policy
----------------

Preserve original behavior and external formats unless a separate,
reviewed compatibility or security change is required. Prefer
isolated regression tests and staged integration testing over a
bulk source rewrite. Keep source comments and documentation in
English, consistent with the original project.


SMTP loopback integration checks
-------------------------------

The integration/test_ofmipd_loopback.py suite starts the original
SMTPServer bound to 127.0.0.1 on an ephemeral port, using a callback
that captures messages in memory instead of invoking a local or remote
MTA. The GitHub Actions runner tests Python 3.13 and Python 3.14 with
temporary pyasyncore and pyasynchat compatibility packages.

The test suite covers EHLO, NOOP, QUIT, rejection before AUTH,
AUTH LOGIN failure and success, AUTH PLAIN success, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO,
DATA, 8-bit message content and SMTP dot transparency.

The checks do not cover STARTTLS, implicit TLS, real password stores,
FD3/checkpassword, message injection, external MTA delivery,
concurrent connections, or sustained SMTP traffic.


TLS integration checks
----------------------

The integration/test_ofmipd_starttls.py and
integration/test_ofmipd_implicit_tls.py suites generate temporary
self-signed certificates with OpenSSL and negotiate TLS entirely on
127.0.0.1. They verify TLS 1.2 minimum configuration, mandatory
STARTTLS before AUTH in --tls on mode, and post-handshake SMTP
commands. STARTTLS authentication and DATA are delivered only to an
in-memory callback. No real MTA or external server is contacted.

The SMTP event loop uses TMDA.SMTPEventLoop and Python's selectors
module without pyasyncore or pyasynchat. Do not interpret passing
isolated tests as production TLS certification.

Pending queues and local delivery
---------------------------------

The integration/test_pending_queues.py suite uses private temporary
directories and mock configuration data. It exercises the legacy
OriginalQueue and MaildirQueue formats, including insertion, lookup,
readback, deletion, exact message ID matching and raw eight-bit data.

The integration/test_local_delivery.py suite writes local Maildir,
mbox and MMDF fixtures without invoking sendmail or accessing a
production mail server. Binary file handling was updated for Python 3.

These tests do not yet establish production delivery behavior under
concurrent access, interrupted writes, full filesystems, NFS, real
user/group ownership, or varied MIME encodings. Maildir queue cleanup
and failure recovery still require additional coverage.

MIME and isolated tmda-inject tests
-----------------------------------

The integration/test_mime_compatibility.py suite verifies parsing of
binary message streams, original eight-bit body octets and multipart
attachment integrity. TMDA.Util.msg_from_file maps input bytes to
latin-1 text before invoking the historical message parser; the
mapping is reversible when the legacy output is encoded to bytes.

The integration/test_tmda_inject.py suite launches the real tmda-inject
entry point with a private TMDA configuration and a local executable
which only captures standard input. No external SMTP connection or
production sendmail daemon is used. Its multipart attachment is
checked after message rewriting and local capture.

Scripts without a .py extension now receive explicit py_compile checks
for tmda-inject, tmda-ofmipd and tmda-keygen. Further executable
entry points still require dedicated runtime and syntax coverage.

These tests do not yet cover internationalized RFC 6532 headers,
malformed MIME, message size limits, multiple outgoing recipients,
real MTA delivery, or all mail transport configurations.

Outgoing SMTP transport regression tests
----------------------------------------

The integration/test_outgoing_smtp.py suite uses mock SMTP connections.
It verifies Python 3 STARTTLS invocation with an SSLContext that
validates remote certificates, EHLO refresh after STARTTLS, optional
client certificate settings, SMTP authentication and message passing.
A plain SMTP connection remains available when SMTPSSL is disabled.

The tmda-inject integration test now also verifies that failed local
sendmail-compatible subprocesses do not produce a successful process
exit status. This is a simulated transport failure, not a production
delivery test.

The outgoing SMTP transport defaults now validate the certificate
chain of the SMTP server when SMTPSSL is enabled. Environments with
private PKI must configure appropriate system trust roots, rather
than relying on the original unverified TLS behavior. Client
certificates require both SMTPSSL_KEYFILE and SMTPSSL_CERTFILE.

Known limitations remain: no live Postfix delivery has been tested,
the SMTP transport's certificate hostname policy has only mock
coverage, and internationalized headers and malformed MIME have not
yet been comprehensively verified.

Selectors-based SMTP event loop
-------------------------------

TMDA/SMTPEventLoop.py provides the socket dispatcher and delimiter-
based chat operations needed by the original tmda-ofmipd. It is
not a general-purpose compatibility implementation of asyncore or
asynchat. The SMTP proxy and isolated tests now use the local module.
Both SMTP GitHub Actions workflows run without installing
pyasyncore or pyasynchat.

The additional tests exercise fragmented command terminators,
SMTP command pipelining, large payloads across socket reads,
output producers, response flushing and concurrent SMTP sessions.
SMTP transport and TLS loopback regressions still run on Python
3.13 and 3.14 without external event loop packages.

Production use remains prohibited until stress testing, protocol
fuzzing, TLS handshake failure handling, socket resource limits,
and integration with actual MTAs are fully validated.

Entry-point and development install audit
-----------------------------------------

The original tmda-pending and tmda-rfilter scripts contained
mixed tab/space indentation that prevented Python 3 startup.
Their indentation was normalized without changing program logic.
All nine extensionless command-line programs now receive explicit
compilation checks, and isolated CLI version checks cover the
programs that support the -V option.

The incoming tmda-rfilter now reads input from stdin.buffer into a
BytesIO stream and passes it through the reversible eight-bit
message parser. Its removed string module calls were replaced with
ordinary string methods.

The new integration/test_tagged_address_cli.py suite generates
and validates keyword and dated tagged addresses through actual
CLI processes using private test configuration and keys.

A minimal pyproject.toml enables a development package installation
into an isolated virtual environment; doc/Python3Install describes
the procedure. GitHub Actions checks the installed commands from
outside the checkout with Python 3.13 and Python 3.14.

The incoming filter has not yet been tested end-to-end against
a real Postfix/qmail recipient, and the complete pending queue
administration CLI still needs functional integration coverage.

Incoming message and pending queue end-to-end checks
---------------------------------------------------

integration/test_incoming_pending_cycle.py exercises the actual
tmda-rfilter and tmda-pending executables with temporary home
directories, configuration and encryption keys. No production mail
account or external SMTP server is used.

The incoming filter places messages on hold; tmda-pending then lists
and deletes them using the original and Maildir queue formats.
A separate release test verifies that a pending message can be
re-injected into a local capture executable, carrying the original
body and the X-TMDA-Confirm-Done and X-TMDA-Released headers.

A simulated sendmail failure must cause tmda-pending to exit with
an error while leaving the original pending file intact. This
test covers loss prevention on rejected local reinjection, not
simultaneous or partial delivery.

The tests discovered and fixed a built-in list shadowing bug in the
incoming filter and decoding failures when optional summary headers
are absent. Further load, concurrency, cleanup and real-MTA
integration testing is required before production use.

Pending queue safety and expiration regression
---------------------------------------------

The OriginalQueue and MaildirQueue validate the historical
numeric timestamp.pid identifier before using it in filesystem
operations. This prevents paths supplied by CLI arguments from
escaping PENDING_DIR through embedded separators or dot segments.

Queue cleanup no longer changes the current working directory of
the hosting process. Both formats are regression-tested to verify
that expired messages are removed, recent messages are preserved,
and the working directory remains unchanged.

These checks run in the integration suite on Python 3.12, 3.13 and
3.14. This is not yet a substitute for validating queue races,
unexpected power loss, NFS storage or MTA interoperability.

Local Postfix MTA integration
----------------------------

.github/workflows/python3-postfix-local.yml installs Postfix on an
ephemeral GitHub Actions Ubuntu runner, configures loopback-only SMTP,
disables external default transport, and creates a dedicated local
recipient. The integration/test_postfix_local.py suite invokes the
real tmda-inject command and checks delivery into the local Postfix
mailbox using both the sendmail program transport and direct SMTP.

The tests verify MIME attachment integrity after delivery and exercise
SMTP recipient rejection. Postfix mailboxes remain owned by the local
recipient and are read through a privilege-separated test snapshot,
not by making mailbox permissions publicly readable.

The workflow covers Python 3.12, 3.13 and 3.14. These results establish
functional interoperability with the runner's Postfix build in a
single-host, local-only test configuration. They do not validate
internet-facing SMTP, authenticated relay, anti-spam policy,
production mailbox permissions, FreeBSD/Postfix or throughput under
load. This branch must not be deployed to production yet.

Postfix end-to-end incoming confirmation
---------------------------------------

The same local-only Postfix CI scenario now exercises receiving
an incoming message through tmda-rfilter, holding it in the
OriginalQueue, and releasing it using tmda-pending. The released
message is confirmed to reach the disposable local Postfix mailbox
with its binary attachment intact.

A separate SMTP delivery test attempts an unknown local recipient
and requires TMDA to propagate Postfix's recipient rejection instead
of reporting success. These integration cases run on Python 3.12,
3.13 and 3.14.

Postfix remains configured with loopback-only listeners, local
recipient delivery and disabled external default transport throughout
these CI tests. This adds real-MTA integration evidence but does not
replace staging tests for a production FreeBSD/Postfix deployment,
authenticated relaying, timeouts, mail queue recovery and concurrency.

Concurrent pending queue writes and failure handling
--------------------------------------------------

OriginalQueue now constructs complete messages in hidden temporary
files within the pending directory. After writing and fsyncing the
file, it publishes the historical timestamp.pid.msg filename with
os.link(), which fails rather than replacing an existing message.
The temporary file is removed in a finally block. Message headers
modified during the operation are restored even on write failure.

MaildirQueue uses separate unpredictable temporary filenames in
Maildir/tmp and publishes the final timestamp.pid.hostname filename
into Maildir/new using the same atomic hard-link mechanism. It no
longer changes the process-wide SIGALRM handler during delivery.
Both queue formats now safely create directories when several
writers encounter a new pending directory at the same time.

The test suite covers failed fsync, failed final hard-link
publication, eight simultaneous writers using the same message ID,
and parallel writes of 32 independent messages. The tests verify
that readers see no partial published files and temporary files
are removed when the write or publish operation raises an exception.

Passing these tests on Python 3.12, 3.13 and 3.14 establishes
basic concurrency and error-path behavior only. It does not
demonstrate crash-safe filesystem directory fsync semantics,
network filesystem guarantees, sustained high throughput,
or durability following sudden power loss.

Legacy auto-response and contrib compatibility audit
---------------------------------------------------

The three original AUTORESPONSE_INCLUDE_SENDER_COPY modes (0, 1,
and 2) are exercised by integration/test_autoresponse_compat.py
through the original AutoResponse generator and MIME serializer.

The optional Python helper scripts under contrib/ retain their
original interfaces and purpose. collectaddys now uses current
mailbox.mbox, mailbox.Maildir and mailbox.MH readers with
email.utils.parseaddr. def2html uses Python 3 comparison and option
parsing, and printdbm/printcdb normalize byte strings for the
original key/value line output. smtp-check-sender retains its
original SMTP status convention with Python 3 print syntax.

The original utility behavior is regression tested with temporary
mailboxes, DBM data and Defaults input in
integration/test_contrib_compatibility.py. All five Python helpers
are included in Python 3 source compilation checks.

The third-party cdb and DNS libraries remain external optional
dependencies, as in TMDA 1.1.12; these tests do not claim they
are available or compatible in every FreeBSD Python environment.
The Perl and shell contrib scripts do not need Python translation.
The historical Python 2 email library remains in the source
archive but is not installed or imported by the Python 3 package.

Checkpassword, virtual users, and legacy filter storage
----------------------------------------------------

Auth.checkpassword fd 3 delivery now uses binary credentials and
passes the descriptor to the child process without closing or
reassigning open descriptors in the parent daemon. The original
checkpassword exit status is preserved. The virtual-user lookup,
configuration-directory lookup, and appended checkpassword program
arguments are exercised independently by Python 3 unit tests.

Legacy filter databases require Python 3 bytes compatibility.
DBM and CDB lookups now decode stored action bytes as latin-1,
while preserving their text-based TMDA action grammar. Mailman
2.0 marshal and 2.1 pickle membership files are opened in binary
mode; Python 2 pickle strings are loaded with reversible latin-1
encoding, matching historic stored data. Pending queue caches
likewise load Python 2 eight-bit pickle strings.

The legacy DBM auto-builder and pickle cache writer no longer
modify the global tempfile.tempdir setting. This prevents one
mail operation from affecting temporary file creation in another,
and fixes a regression revealed by running the complete test
suite with temporary directories. Cache writes use a temporary
file in the destination directory and preserve pickle protocol
2 by default.

These changes are compatibility and reliability corrections,
not additional user-visible features. They do not constitute
full production verification of optional third-party cdb,
DNS or LDAP modules, or of original FreeBSD hosting setups.

Legacy ezmlm subscriber files
----------------------------

The from-ezmlm/to-ezmlm filter sources now open subscriber files
with reversible latin-1 decoding rather than the Python 3 default
locale encoding. This preserves original eight-bit subscriber
addresses and NUL-separated record boundaries. The integration
suite includes a legacy subscriber file with a non-ASCII byte.
